  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1014 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nMoisture levels are high for today with dew points this morning in \nthe low-mid 60s. Even some patchy low clouds/fog developed around \nsunrise before mixing out. The persistent stratus deck should \ninhibit storm development this afternoon along with the upper \nridge/warmer temps aloft poking into the southern High Plains from \nthe Ozarks region. If convection pops later today, PWs in the 1.3-\n1.5\" range (90-95th %-ile for early Sep) would allow for high rain \nrates and isolated flash flooding. Areas W of the Divide are more \nlikely to heat up this afternoon as clouds clear with a higher \nthreat of heavy rainfall. \n\nThe most likely scenario depicted by CAMs is storm coverage remains \nlimited through the afternoon (10-30% chances), followed by more \nscattered coverage around midnight, focusing over TX. Isolated \nrainfall over 1\" is possible tonight for El Paso and Hudspeth \nCounties. Gusty outflows up to 40 mph are a secondary threat.\n\nAbove-normal moisture remains for Sat as PWs fall to 1.2-1.3\" and \nwarmer air aloft limits our instability and storm intensity. Like \ntoday, storm chances will depend on how much cloud cover/showers \nstick around Sat AM and keep us from warming up. Generally isolated \ncoverage is forecast for now with brief downpours and gusty winds \nbeing the main hazards on Sat.\n\nThe upper high sits over the central/southern Plains through \nmidweek, focusing the best monsoonal moisture over western \nNM/eastern AZ. Storm chances and the heavy rain risk will be \nelevated generally W of the Divide and over the Sacs from Sun \nonward. More spotty coverage is forecast along the RGV and east as \nPWs hold around 1.2\", which is around the 75th %-ile. Any \nshortwaves/vort maxes moving up the RGV around the upper high will \ndetermine any upticks in storm chances next week. \n\nTemperatures will be near or slightly above normal through the \nperiod with lowland highs in the low-mid 90s. Relatively cool temps \nare forecast out west today further from the high pressure aloft, \nwarming up for the weekend.",
